WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:05.460
The "House of the Dragon" season finale was&nbsp;
pretty brutal, but it could have been terrifying.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.080
The first season of the "Game of Thrones"&nbsp;
prequel includes several intense birth scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.100
that emphasize the brutal nature&nbsp;
of childbirth in Westeros. Indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.780
the very first episode features a particularly&nbsp;
violent and bloody scene in which Queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.520
Aemma's labor goes horribly wrong — and&nbsp;
neither the mother nor the baby survives.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:29.460
Episode 6 features two more birth scenes, one&nbsp;
in which Princess Rhaenyra Targaryen delivers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.504
her baby and then immediately marches&nbsp;
through the Red Keep, trailing blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.504 --> 00:00:39.144
and another in which Laena Velaryon commits&nbsp;
suicide-by-dragon after her own labor goes awry.

00:00:39.251 --> 00:00:41.351
"I've reached the limit of my art."

00:00:41.694 --> 00:00:46.740
Yet the series saves its most disturbing birth&nbsp;
scene for the Season 1 finale, in which we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:52.500
Rhaenyra enter labor prematurely and deliver&nbsp;
a deformed, stillborn baby named Visenya.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.520
Although the camera doesn't linger too&nbsp;
long on baby Visenya within the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.820
a recent Instagram post from&nbsp;
the series' prosthetic makeup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.622
designer Barrie Gower gives us a&nbsp;
closer look at the baby's design.

00:01:03.622 --> 00:01:07.740
Within the episode itself, it's hard to get a&nbsp;
good look at the stillborn Visenya as she is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.720
covered in blood and held tightly in the arms of&nbsp;
Rhaenyra — but Gower's post illuminates some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:18.600
the finer details, with two brightly-lit images&nbsp;
of baby Visenya against a black background. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.640
first one highlights her deformed nose and sunken&nbsp;
face, and the second shows her cone-shaped skull.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:28.800
The most striking deformity present in these two&nbsp;
images is the numerous dragon-like scales that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.180
cover Visenya's head, chest, and back.&nbsp;
The second image shows that she has an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.560
abundance of these scales on the back of her&nbsp;
head and running down her spine — and that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:42.000
back of her head features an almost horn-like&nbsp;
protrusion that accounts for her skull's shape.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.439
Considering the immense amount of&nbsp;
detail shown in this small model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:49.093
it's surprising that these scales are&nbsp;
so obscured in the episode itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.093 --> 00:01:54.060
especially since they are specifically mentioned&nbsp;
in the series' source material, "Fire &amp; Blood."

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.140
In the "Fire &amp; Blood" chapter "The&nbsp;
Dying of the Dragons," a fool known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:02.580
as Mushroom claims that Rhaenyra cursed&nbsp;
baby Visenya during her premature birth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:07.200
calling her a "monster" and yelling&nbsp;
for her to "get out." Mushroom goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.400
on to allege that these curses bore fruit&nbsp;
when the Visenya was finally born, saying,

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.880
"When the babe at last came forth,&nbsp;
she proved indeed a monster:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.820
a stillborn girl, twisted and malformed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:22.260
with a hole in her chest where her heart&nbsp;
should have been, and a stubby, scaled tail."

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.880
It's worth mentioning that Mushroom's account of&nbsp;
the events in "Fire &amp; Blood" is considered to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.740
unreliable and exaggerated. So it makes sense&nbsp;
that the design team chose to tone down his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:36.240
version of Visenya for "House of the Dragon,"&nbsp;
while still making her somewhat dragon-like.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.420
Although this silicone model does&nbsp;
not appear to include the tail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:44.340
it's clear that the prosthetics team paid&nbsp;
close attention to the details of Visenya's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:49.380
malformed appearance and scales — culminating&nbsp;
in the design we see in Gower's Instagram post.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.460
It's hard to grasp the immense attention&nbsp;
to detail that went into this model when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:58.680
watching the episode itself due to Visenya's&nbsp;
incredibly brief appearance — though Gower's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.760
post makes it abundantly it's clear that the&nbsp;
prosthetics department went above and beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:07.180
to make this final birth scene really stand&nbsp;
out amongst all the others in the series.

